\page page_continuous_integration Continuous Integration (CI)

\tableofcontents

## Gitlab CI

For a full overview of how continuous integration works in gitlab, please consult [their documentation](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/).
We detail here some useful info about how we use it within Peano. A few basic things to note:

\li When a developer pushes to any remote branch, the gitlab website "runs a pipeline". This consists of running any steps in the `.gitlab-ci.yml` file.
\li Our ```.gitlab-ci.yml``` doesn't contain much actual stuff, but rather it redirects to further `.yml` files in the `.continuous_integration` directory.
\li We use containers to make sure we have a dependable software environment.
\li Most runner nodes have no internet access.
\li Every job clones the Git LFS files as pointers only (`GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE` in `.continuous_integration/templates/variables.yml`). A job that reads Git LFS files pulls just those with `git lfs pull --include="<pattern>"`.
\li Before a job fetches the sources, it deletes the Git configuration lock file that a cancelled job leaves in the reused build directory. GitLab repeats a job up to twice after an infrastructure failure and a failed fetch, artifact download or cache restore up to three times.

## How do I run the pipeline?

The pipeline runs automatically for all branches and all pushes.
However, it only runs fully on the protected branches.
For all other branches, the pipeline only executes the build and test (unit tests) stages.

At time of writing, ```gitlab-runner``` runs as Slurm jobs on COSMA: one permanent runner on the node ga004 of the partition `cosma8-shm2`
and on-demand runners on the DINE nodes of the partition `bluefield1`, which the permanent runner submits when all of its job slots are busy.
Every runner keeps its configuration, build directories and container images in one runner directory `<runner-dir>` on `/cosma/apps`, which every runner node mounts.
Recurring Slurm jobs keep the runners alive. Peano developers should not need to do
anything other than push to a branch, and [check the pipelines page on the gitlab](https://gitlab.lrz.de/hpcsoftware/Peano/-/pipelines)
to check that their code has correctly built for each compiler toolchain.

One unfortunate consequence of this is that if the slurm job that manages a particular runner goes down during a pipeline run, it will cause the jobs to hang
until they timeout, meaning that whoever pushed needs to press the restart button on the pipeline page.

## How do I write my own tests?

Unfortunately, the use of the apptainer containers is not pretty, and
so the best thing to do is describe your desired actions in a bash script and ask for help on the
continuous-integration channel of the Slack.

### Tests driven by pytest

The preferred way to add a test is to write it as a pytest test in `tests/` (see
\ref tarch_pytest) and to let the CI job call pytest. The container images ship pytest.
A job then reads

```
.My_Test:
  script:
    - apptainer exec -e --bind "$APPTAINER_BINDDIR" $APPTAINER_IMAGEDIR/${IMAGE_TAG} python3 -m pytest -m integration tests/exaparty -k sod-1d
```

The jobs `Python_Unit_Tests` (all fast tests, i.e. plain `pytest`) and
`ExaParty_*` in `.continuous_integration/templates/exaparty.yml` follow this pattern.
`Python_Unit_Tests` does not need the `Build` job, as the unit tests do not compile
any code.

### Anatomy of a test

It's best to copy examples, such as the ones in `.continuous_integration/templates/exahype2.yml`. But here are some general tips:

```
.Test_Name:
  stage: Test
  needs:
    - job: Build
  script:
    - apptainer exec -e --bind "$APPTAINER_BINDDIR" $APPTAINER_IMAGEDIR/${IMAGE_TAG} bash <<< "cd to/my/directory; ./run_my_executable"
```

Let's discuss this line-by-line.

Start by giving your test a name. Syntax is crucial here. Start with a `.` to indicate it's a template, and finish the line with a colon `:`. Every
subsequent line must be indented with two spaces, python style.

Next, you need to give the job a stage. `Test` is a fine name.

Next, you need to make sure that this job depends on the build job. That way, it will run strictly after the job that checks it still compiles, and only
if that succeeded. What's more, you won't have to wait for the whole of Peano to recompile; it will just fetch the compiled libraries that were cooked up
a short while beforehand.

Finally, the script. This is very ugly. The first part `apptainer exec -e --bind "$APPTAINER_BINDDIR" $APPTAINER_IMAGEDIR/${IMAGE_TAG} bash` just transforms
the standard bash terminal into one that runs under the container. The names of the environment variables are set for you. Then we need to pipe in our instructions
(note the triple `<` sign). Conceptually, you need to imagine that the terminal begins at the top level of Peano. So you need to `cd` into whichever directory you
need to be and run whichever scripts you need to run. Note the separation with semicolon between separate statements, like in a normal bash terminal.


## How do I add new runners to project?

Having more runners online at once means more jobs can be run at once. Only the project owner can do these steps:

\li Heading to Settings > CI/CD > Runners
\li Add a new project runner. Make sure the tag is set to `peano-dine-runner` (no other tags have been written into the repo yet), make sure the project is locked to the current project and click create.

It will then show you a token. Write it down.

Now we need to register this runner. Log into a node where `gitlab-runner` is available, and then type `gitlab-runner register`. Type in the url and
token as prompted. Pick `shell` as an executor.

This will then create a new config file in `$HOME/.gitlab-runner` directory. Move this to the `<runner-dir>/configs` directory,
add the paths of the config template below and list the runner with its node, partition and mode in `runners.txt` there.

You can then run the gitlab-runner and supply this config by calling `gitlab-runner run --config /path/to/config.toml`. This is what the slurm job is supposed to do.

### Config template

\verbatim

concurrent = 4
check_interval = 0
shutdown_timeout = 0

[session_server]
  session_timeout = 1800

[[runners]]
  name = "b101Runner"
  url = "https://gitlab.lrz.de"
  id = 35483
  token = "keep-this-token-private"
  token_obtained_at = 2024-06-19T11:16:06Z
  token_expires_at = 0001-01-01T00:00:00Z
  executor = "shell"
  builds_dir = "<runner-dir>/builds_dir"
  cache_dir  = "<runner-dir>/cache_dir"
  environment = ["APPTAINER_BINDDIR=<runner-dir>", "APPTAINER_IMAGEDIR=<runner-dir>/images"]

\endverbatim

concurrent denotes the number of concurrent builds that can run at once.
environment sets the directory that Apptainer binds into the containers and the directory of the container images.

*/
